1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,293
This is ECE 320, video 8. The topic is the basics of pipelining.

2
00:00:06,743 --> 00:00:28,013
After this video, you can solve four types of problems. You can compare the time per instruction of three designs. You can find the clock period of a pipeline. You can calculate the speedup of pipelining, with overhead and with stalls. And you can draw a pipeline diagram and find the run time of a short program.

3
00:00:28,463 --> 00:00:47,919
Start with the single-cycle datapath of slide 3. It fetches, decodes and executes one complete instruction every cycle. Here, each part of the datapath takes about 10 nanoseconds. So the load word instruction needs all 5 parts, and 50 nanoseconds.

4
00:00:48,369 --> 00:00:55,238
The add instruction does not use the data memory. But it still gets the full 50 nanosecond cycle.

5
00:00:55,688 --> 00:01:01,107
The store word instruction does not write a register. It also gets the full cycle.

6
00:01:01,557 --> 00:01:11,114
So the single-cycle design has a CPI of 1. But the clock period must fit the slowest instruction. That is 50 nanoseconds.

7
00:01:11,564 --> 00:01:25,836
Now look at the instruction memory. It works for 10 nanoseconds, then waits 40 nanoseconds. Every other part is also idle for 4 fifths of each cycle. Pipelining will use this idle time.

8
00:01:26,286 --> 00:01:38,937
The multi-cycle datapath of slide 4 attacks the slow clock. One instruction uses several short cycles. Micro-coded control sets the control signals of each step.

9
00:01:39,387 --> 00:01:53,957
The main point: each instruction uses only the cycles that it needs. With an 11 nanosecond clock, a load takes 5 cycles, an ALU instruction 4 cycles, and a branch 3 cycles.

10
00:01:54,407 --> 00:02:12,093
The mix on slide 5 is 20 percent branches, 20 percent loads, and 60 percent ALU instructions. So the CPI is point 2 times 3, plus point 2 times 5, plus point 6 times 4. The CPI is 4.

11
00:02:12,543 --> 00:02:30,292
The time per instruction is the clock period times the CPI. That is 11 times 4, which is 44 nanoseconds. The single-cycle design needs 50. So multi-cycle has the opposite split: a short clock, but a high CPI.

12
00:02:30,742 --> 00:02:44,950
Slide 5 asks why the clock is 11 nanoseconds and not 10. Each step ends in a register, and the register adds overhead to every cycle. Remember this overhead. Pipelining has it too.

13
00:02:45,400 --> 00:02:59,800
Here is the single-cycle design on a time line. Three instructions take 3 times 50, which is 150 nanoseconds. Inside each instruction, the 5 parts work one after the other.

14
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:15,162
Pipelining cuts the datapath into 5 stages. When instruction 1 leaves the first stage, instruction 2 enters it. So up to 5 instructions are in the datapath at the same time, one in each stage.

15
00:03:15,612 --> 00:03:25,809
The first instruction finishes after 5 cycles. After that, one instruction finishes in every cycle. The green dots mark the finish times.

16
00:03:26,259 --> 00:03:43,859
Each stage ends in a pipeline register, and the register adds overhead. Slide 13 uses about 2 nanoseconds. So the clock is 10 plus 2, which is 12 nanoseconds. Three instructions need 7 cycles: 84 nanoseconds.

17
00:03:44,309 --> 00:03:56,384
The rule: cut the datapath into 5 stages, and start a new instruction in every cycle. The pipeline fills in 4 cycles. Then one instruction finishes per cycle.

18
00:03:56,834 --> 00:04:15,138
Slide 8 cuts the datapath into five stages. Fetch, F, reads the instruction memory. Decode, D, reads the register file. Execute, X, uses the ALU. Memory, M, uses the data memory. Writeback, W, writes the register file.

19
00:04:15,588 --> 00:04:30,500
Between two stages, there is a pipeline register. It is named by the two stages it separates: F slash D, D slash X, X slash M, and M slash W. The PC is the register before fetch.

20
00:04:30,950 --> 00:04:46,950
Why are these registers necessary? Up to 5 instructions are in the pipeline at once, with different PCs. This is the snapshot of slide 9. Each pipeline register holds the values of the instruction in the next stage.

21
00:04:47,400 --> 00:05:04,979
The instruction bits must travel with the data, so that control operates correctly in each stage. This is pipelined control. Slide 8 also notes that the M stage computes PC plus 4 again. So only the PC travels down the pipeline.

22
00:05:05,429 --> 00:05:13,407
Now watch the instructions of slide 9 move through the pipeline. In cycle 1, the add instruction is in fetch.

23
00:05:13,857 --> 00:05:18,764
In cycle 2, the add moves to decode. The or instruction enters fetch.

24
00:05:19,214 --> 00:05:26,297
In cycle 3, the add is in execute, the or in decode, and the set less than instruction in fetch.

25
00:05:26,747 --> 00:05:31,568
In cycle 4, the store word enters fetch. Four stages are busy.

26
00:05:32,018 --> 00:05:48,807
In cycle 5, all five stages are busy. This is the snapshot of slide 9. This sequence has dependences on t0 and t3. Video 9 shows how the pipeline handles them. Here, we look only at the positions.

27
00:05:49,257 --> 00:05:59,945
So in each cycle, every instruction moves one stage to the right. At the clock edge, each pipeline register takes the values of the stage on its left.

28
00:06:00,395 --> 00:06:13,729
A pipeline diagram is a short way to show instructions moving through the pipeline. Across are the cycles. Down are the instructions, in program order. This is the example of slide 12.

29
00:06:14,179 --> 00:06:20,813
In cycle 1, the add is fetched. In cycle 2, the add is in decode, and the load word is fetched.

30
00:06:21,263 --> 00:06:36,559
In cycle 4, the load word is in X. The convention: a letter in a cycle means the instruction finishes that stage at the end of that cycle. So the load writes its X result into X slash M at the end of cycle 4.

31
00:06:37,009 --> 00:06:44,455
The store word finishes in cycle 7. One instruction finishes in each of the cycles 5, 6 and 7.

32
00:06:44,905 --> 00:06:59,987
Without stalls, N instructions need N plus 4 cycles. The first instruction needs 5 cycles. Each other instruction adds 1 cycle. Also, each column has at most one instruction in each stage.

33
00:07:00,437 --> 00:07:14,837
Slide 10 splits the single-cycle path into its delays: the instruction memory, the register file, the ALU, the data memory, and the register file again. Their sum is the single-cycle clock period.

34
00:07:15,287 --> 00:07:29,453
In the pipeline, all stages use one clock. So every stage gets the same time. The slowest stage sets this time. The other stages finish early, and wait. The widths here are only an example.

35
00:07:29,903 --> 00:07:40,847
Each stage also ends in a pipeline register. The register adds overhead to every clock period. Assignment 3 calls this overhead clock skew and setup.

36
00:07:41,297 --> 00:07:54,395
So the clock period is the maximum of the stage delays, plus the overhead. The base CPI is 1. The actual CPI is more than 1, because the pipeline must often stall.

37
00:07:54,845 --> 00:08:06,323
The latency of one instruction is now 5 clock periods. That is longer than the single-cycle period. The slide says this is OK. The next slide shows why.

38
00:08:06,773 --> 00:08:17,567
Compare the two designs on one time line, with the numbers of slide 13. The single-cycle design finishes one instruction every 50 nanoseconds.

39
00:08:18,017 --> 00:08:30,497
The pipelined design has a 12 nanosecond clock. The first instruction needs 5 cycles, so it finishes at 60 nanoseconds. Its latency is longer than 50.

40
00:08:30,947 --> 00:08:42,659
But the next instructions finish at 72, 84 and 96 nanoseconds. One instruction finishes every 12 nanoseconds. So the throughput is much better.

41
00:08:43,109 --> 00:08:53,371
This is the summary of slide 59. Pipelining improves throughput, not latency. It usually increases the latency of each instruction.

42
00:08:53,821 --> 00:09:09,885
Slide 11 gives three terms. A scalar pipeline has at most one instruction in each stage in each cycle. Look at column 4: one F, one D and one X. The alternative, superscalar, comes later in the course.

43
00:09:10,335 --> 00:09:20,425
An in-order pipeline sends instructions into the execute stage in program order. The alternative is out-of-order, also later in the course.

44
00:09:20,875 --> 00:09:35,425
The pipeline depth is the number of stages. Our pipeline has depth 5. Nothing is special about five. The Pentium 4 had 22 stages. The trend was to deeper pipelines, but this trend has slowed.

45
00:09:35,875 --> 00:09:48,035
Slide 13 compares the three designs. The single-cycle design has a 50 nanosecond clock and a CPI of 1. That is 50 nanoseconds per instruction.

46
00:09:48,485 --> 00:09:57,829
The multi-cycle design has an 11 nanosecond clock and a CPI of 4. That is 44 nanoseconds per instruction.

47
00:09:58,279 --> 00:10:15,751
The 5-stage pipeline has a clock of about 50 divided by 5, plus overheads. That is 12 nanoseconds. Each instruction takes 5 cycles, but one completes every cycle. So the CPI is 1, and the time is 12 nanoseconds.

48
00:10:16,201 --> 00:10:34,505
In practice, the CPI is 1 plus a penalty for stalls. Slide 13 uses a CPI of 1.5. So the time is 12 times 1.5, which is 18 nanoseconds per instruction. The red part is the stall cost.

49
00:10:34,955 --> 00:10:47,712
Even with stalls, the pipeline is much faster than the other two designs. For all three, use the same formula: time per instruction equals the clock period times the CPI.

50
00:10:48,162 --> 00:11:05,101
Now a practice question, assignment 3, question 1 a. The table gives the CPI and the fraction of each class. The clock is 10 nanoseconds. Pipelining adds 1 nanosecond of overhead. Find the speedup. Pause the video and try it.

51
00:11:05,551 --> 00:11:21,892
First, the non-pipelined time. The CPI is point 5 times 4, plus point 2 times 4, plus point 3 times 5. That is 4.3. Times 10 nanoseconds, that is 43 I C nanoseconds.

52
00:11:22,342 --> 00:11:28,230
The bar shows one average instruction: 4.3 cycles of 10 nanoseconds.

53
00:11:28,680 --> 00:11:42,163
In the pipeline, the CPI is 1, because there are no hazards. The clock becomes 10 plus 1, which is 11 nanoseconds. So the pipelined time is 11 I C nanoseconds.

54
00:11:42,613 --> 00:11:52,255
The speedup is 43 divided by 11, which is 3.91. The I C cancels, because both run the same program.

55
00:11:52,705 --> 00:12:11,095
Question 1 b has a second processor. The clock is 1 nanosecond, and pipelining adds point 3 nanoseconds. Part i has no stalls. In part ii, a hazard causes a 5-cycle stall 2 percent of the time. Pause the video and try it.

56
00:12:11,545 --> 00:12:27,353
The non-pipelined CPI is point 5 times 3, plus point 1 times 4, plus point 4 times 5. That is 3.9. With a 1 nanosecond clock, the time is 3.9 I C nanoseconds.

57
00:12:27,803 --> 00:12:39,899
Part i. The pipelined CPI is 1, and the clock is 1.3 nanoseconds. The speedup is 3.9 divided by 1.3, which is 3.

58
00:12:40,349 --> 00:13:01,405
Part ii. The stall adds 2 percent times 5 cycles to the CPI. So the CPI is 1.1. The time is 1.1 times 1.3, which is 1.43. The speedup is 3.9 divided by 1.43, which is 2.73.

59
00:13:01,855 --> 00:13:10,175
The rule: stalls add to the CPI. The CPI is 1, plus the stall frequency times the stall cycles.

60
00:13:10,625 --> 00:13:29,483
Assignment 3, question 2 a. A three-line program, store word, add, subtract, runs on the 5-stage pipeline. Fill in the pipeline diagram, with no hazards. Then find the run time with the 1.3 nanosecond clock. Pause the video and try it.

61
00:13:29,933 --> 00:13:39,064
The store word starts in cycle 1. Each next instruction starts one cycle later. Each row is F, D, X, M, W.

62
00:13:39,514 --> 00:13:46,319
In cycle 5, the store word is in W. The add is in M, and the subtract is in X.

63
00:13:46,769 --> 00:13:53,980
The subtract finishes W in cycle 7. So the program takes 3 plus 4, which is 7 cycles.

64
00:13:54,430 --> 00:14:00,915
The run time is 7 times 1.3 nanoseconds, which is 9.1 nanoseconds.

65
00:14:01,365 --> 00:14:18,560
Parts b and c of question 2. The pipelined run time is 9.1 nanoseconds, from part a. Now run the same program on the non-pipelined processor of question 1 b. Then explain the speedup. Pause the video and try it.

66
00:14:19,010 --> 00:14:36,866
On the non-pipelined processor, the store word is a memory instruction. It takes 5 cycles. The add and the subtract are ALU instructions. Each takes 3 cycles. So the time is 11 cycles of 1 nanosecond: 11 nanoseconds.

67
00:14:37,316 --> 00:14:45,828
The speedup is 11 divided by 9.1, which is only 1.21. In question 1 b, it was 3.

68
00:14:46,278 --> 00:15:04,262
The reason: 4 of the 7 pipelined cycles only fill the pipeline. The program is so short that this initial latency dominates. Pipelines improve throughput, not latency. In a long program, the fill time is small, and the speedup is larger.

69
00:15:04,712 --> 00:15:25,107
Assignment 3, question 3 looks at the pipeline depth. Every instruction takes 50 nanoseconds without pipelining. A pipeline of depth d divides the work evenly. In part a, there is a fixed 1 nanosecond overhead. In part b, there is no overhead. Pause the video and try it.

70
00:15:25,557 --> 00:15:43,434
With a depth of d, each stage gets 50 divided by d nanoseconds of work. Add the 1 nanosecond overhead. The speedup is 50 divided by this time. For d equal to 2, that is 50 divided by 26, which is 1.92.

71
00:15:43,884 --> 00:15:58,369
For d equal to 10, each stage gets 5 nanoseconds. Add 1, which gives 6. The speedup is 50 divided by 6, which is 8.33. The overhead is now one sixth of the clock.

72
00:15:58,819 --> 00:16:07,651
Part b. With no overhead, the speedup is 50 divided by 50 over d, which is d. So the speedups are 2 and 10.

73
00:16:08,101 --> 00:16:28,133
Part c asks for the speedup with an infinite number of stages. Pause the video and try it. With the overhead, 50 over d goes to 0. The clock period goes to 1 nanosecond. So the speedup goes to 50 divided by 1, which is 50. The blue curve flattens at 50.

74
00:16:28,583 --> 00:16:35,005
With no overhead, the speedup equals d. So it grows without a limit. That is the dashed line.

75
00:16:35,455 --> 00:16:44,393
The lesson: the overhead of the pipeline registers does not shrink when you add stages. So it limits the gain of deeper pipelines.

76
00:16:44,843 --> 00:17:00,033
Here is the recap. One. The time per instruction is the clock period times the CPI. Single-cycle has a long clock and a CPI of 1. Multi-cycle has a short clock and a high CPI.

77
00:17:00,483 --> 00:17:07,800
Two. The pipeline has 5 stages: F, D, X, M and W. Pipeline registers separate them.

78
00:17:08,250 --> 00:17:14,031
Three. The instruction bits travel with the data, so each stage has the correct control.

79
00:17:14,481 --> 00:17:26,023
Four. The clock period is the slowest stage plus the register overhead. The base CPI is 1. Stalls add the stall frequency times the stall cycles.

80
00:17:26,473 --> 00:17:36,862
Five. In a pipeline diagram, cycles go across and instructions go down. Without stalls, N instructions need N plus 4 cycles.

81
00:17:37,312 --> 00:17:45,483
Six. Pipelining improves throughput, not latency. A short program gains little, because the fill time dominates.

82
00:17:45,933 --> 00:18:05,239
Seven. With an even split into d stages, the speedup is the old time divided by the old time over d plus the overhead. Its limit is the old time divided by the overhead. The next video shows what happens when instructions depend on each other. That is the end of video 8.
